  "reconstructibility": 2,
  "reason": "R3 is highly anticipatory: it studies annealing near 900 K in PdCuPtNiCo nanoparticles, differing from AuCoCuNiPt by only Pd/Au substitution, and directly predicts strong Cu and noble-metal surface segregation. R4–R6 and R8 supply precedent for high-temperature treatment and atomistic analysis of multicomponent nanoparticles, while R11, R12, R14, R18, R19, and R20 frame diffusion and stability as kinetically controlled rather than guaranteed by configurational entropy. Together with the Au-containing nanoparticle synthesis in R5, an expert could plausibly propose heating the closely analogous AuCoCuNiPt nanoparticles and expect mobile Au/Cu to segregate toward the surface. The exact vacuum in-situ ramp, reshaping-enhanced diffusion, selective evaporation, and disappearance of the smallest particles are not directly disclosed, but they are refinements rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
}
